a new statement there from msghani what  can we make of that  i think the statement from nazareth  ghani there probably raises uh two main  issues one is sort of technical and to  do with process and the other is to do  with um culture i think on the technical  side  it clearly uh sets out i think what  we've been reporting this morning and  what guests we'd had on um people that  had served say in the in the  conservative muslim forum have been  telling us  that there was really a lack of faith  that the actual disciplinary processes  the complaints processes that were  available to pursue issues like this so  that would have been the complaints  process that conservative party  headquarters would have run really  didn't have the confidence  of  nusgarni that it was  robust enough to deal with the  seriousness of the accusations that she  was making and clearly she felt  that it was not appropriate  for  the party to be essentially  investigating something that a  government minister um she said um had  uh told her  the second point i think that comes out  of that statement from miss ghani um is  about how complaints of this type are  treated within government  and whether um the government can fully  understand all the people that these  complaints are made to can fully  understand uh the hurt really that is  caused to someone of muslim faith when  they are essentially  told as nazgani alleges  that that is a reason  for her not progressing within her  career because ghani is essentially  saying that yes the prime minister did  meet with her yes he did invite her to  pursue this formal complaints process  but in her view he didn't believe that  the reaction from the prime minister and  the reaction from the government matched  the seriousness of what she was saying  and the impact that it had had on her  professionally um and personally now  what of course we've heard from downing  street and also from um mark spencer the  chief whip who is alleged to have made  these comments they have all said that  there is a zero tolerance approach  towards islamophobia within the  conservative party uh but they have also  said that it should be for the official  conservative party  complaints channel to deal with  accusations like this so i think nusgani  would say that that proves a point that  this isn't being taken  seriously enough  what we have also seen today that i  think is interesting  is the reaction that we've had in the  last 24 hours after this story broke in  the sunday times from cabinet ministers  and also from senior mps now late last  night when um the response was  officially coming in from the government  whips office that essentially was a  blanket denial and said that nurse  garnish needed to make a complaint if  there was going to be an investigation  and until she did that there wouldn't be  an inquiry nadim zahawi the education  secretary was tweeting that he thought  there now should be an investigation we  have also got quite a strongly worded  tweet given his position within the  cabinet from the health secretary  sanchez javid  today he has said nusgani is a friend  and a credit to the conservative party  this is a very serious matter which  needs a proper investigation i would  strongly support her in making a formal  complaint  she must be heard so sanji javid saying  essentially the onus is on nusgani to  make that complaint but i think when you  contrast what he has said there to the  quite robust denial from his ministerial  colleague mark spencer in a series of  late night tweets last night and then  you throw into the mix what the  education secretary said it's hard not  to see a degree of cabinet fracturing  over this now it would be easy to i  think dismiss that or not dismiss it but  to put that down to the seriousness of  these accusations if it wasn't for other  issues such as national insurance and  tax rises covert restrictions where we  have seen senior members we know about  senior members of boris johnson's team  beginning to push back  against the prime minister and express  their opinions a bit more  fervently we have also seen i think  arguments breaking out further down  government ranks and within the party  that i think at a time of stability in  government may well have been closed  down by the prime minister that are now  not being closed down and i think you  put all that together and when you speak  to mps that are skeptical about the  future of boris johnson they say that  this is what gives them um  their uh evidence if you like for boris  johnson not being able to survive impost  for much longer because they say the  instability the ripples that have  essentially been sent out by the  partygate um scandal and the impact that  has had on his authority sort of  draining authority from him  means that the process of governing is  becoming a lot more bumpy than it was so  when you're faced with really really  important and serious issues like the  one that nurse ghani has raised like  we'll face in april when it comes to  national insurance tax rises or energy  rises the authority that the prime  minister should have to sort of carry  his party and his administration with  him starts to go and i think what  skeptical rebel mps will say is that  when that's gone it's difficult to get  back and the only solution really is to  change the person at the top
 

